the UN calls the war in Yemen the  world's worst humanitarian crisis peace  talks aimed at ending the conflict are  showing small signs of progress more  than three years of fighting has killed  more than 10,000 people about 14 million  others are at risk of starvation  Elizabeth Palmer is the only u.s.  Network television correspondent in  Yemen she went to Aden to visit an  American group working to help children  there good morning the signs coming out  of the peace talks may be encouraging  but clearly this process is going to  take a very long time meanwhile Yemenis  have no choice but to struggle on the  most routine things like school are hard  one it's hard to believe now but three  years ago the abbas al husseiny school  was overrun by Houthi fighters they  turned it into a prison until they were  pushed out now the American aid  organization Mercy Corps has renovated  it over 600 students study here  including some who fled the fighting  further away like Riyadh do you know  anything about the peace talks that are  going on now between the Houthis and the  governments yes clearly not like most  teenage boys he's got more practical  things to worry about his studies for  example Riyadh told us he wants to be a  doctor the war has turned aid into a  lifeline in Yemen nearby Mercy Corps is  paying 70 men to install a solar-powered  water pump their salaries from this job  are all that stand between their  families the United Nations says that  three out of four Yemenis need aid right  now and yet millions aren't getting it  because of the fighting they're the ones  who are facing serious hunger this  winter for CBS this morning  I'm Elizabeth Palmer  Aiden
 

